/-!
# Degree zero of the section ring, and the global functions acting on twisted sections

Let `X` be a scheme and `L` an invertible `𝒪_X`-module. The section ring
`Γ_*(X, L) = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} (𝟙_ ⟶ L ^⊗ n)` of [Stacks, Tag 01CV] is
`Stacks.GradedSections.sectionRing L`, and its degree-zero piece `powSections L 0` is the
endomorphism group `(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ 𝟙_ X.Modules)` of the monoidal unit, which
`Stacks.SectionRingGeometric.unitScalarAddEquiv` identifies with the ring `Γ(X, ⊤)` of global
functions. This file assembles out of that identification the structural input that
[Stacks, Tag 0B5T] clause (5) needs: **the degree-zero inclusion `Γ(X, ⊤) →+* Γ_*(X, L)` is a
ring homomorphism, and the `Γ_*(X, L)`-action on the graded module of twisted sections
`Γ_*(X, L, ℱ) = ⨁_{m ≥ 0} (𝟙_ ⟶ ℱ ⊗ L ^⊗ m)` restricts in degree zero to the ordinary
`Γ(X, ⊤)`-module structure on global sections.**

The second statement is the one with content. The `Γ(X, ⊤)`-module structure put on a hom group
`(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ N)` here is precomposition with the endomorphism of the monoidal unit that the
scalar names, and `Stacks.SectionRingZero.schemeGlobalSectionsLinearEquiv` is the assertion that
the carrier comparison `Stacks.SectionRingGeometric.schemeGlobalSectionsAddEquiv` — an additive
isomorphism `(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ N) ≃+ Γ(N, ⊤)` — is `Γ(X, ⊤)`-linear for it. That is
`Stacks.SectionRingGeometric.globalSection_unitComp`, i.e. the statement that the unit-hom
correspondence of a sheaf of modules carries composition with a scalar endomorphism to scalar
multiplication of sections.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.unitHomModule`: the `Γ(X, ⊤)`-module structure on
  `(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ N)`, by precomposition with the scalar endomorphism of the monoidal unit.
* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.schemeGlobalSectionsLinearEquiv`: the carrier comparison
  `(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ N) ≃ₗ[Γ(X, ⊤)] Γ(N, ⊤)`.
* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.sectionRingZeroHom`: the degree-zero inclusion
  `Γ(X, ⊤) →+* Γ_*(X, L)`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.of_zero_smul_of`: **the degree-zero compatibility.** A global function
  acting through `sectionRingZeroHom` on a homogeneous twisted section is its ordinary action on
  that section.
* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.of_mem_span`: the `Γ(X, ⊤)`-submodule of
  `Stacks.TwistedSections.twistSections F L p` generated by a set `S` is carried by
  `DirectSum.of _ p` into the `Γ_*(X, L)`-submodule of `Stacks.TwistedSections.twistModule F L`
  generated by the image of `S`.
* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.moduleFinite_unitHom`: for `f : X ⟶ Spec R` proper over a Noetherian
  ring and `N` coherent, `(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ N)` is a finite `Γ(X, ⊤)`-module; this is clause (3)
  of [Stacks, Tag 0B5T] in degree zero, transported along the carrier comparison.
* `Stacks.SectionRingZero.exists_finset_span_eq_top`: the same, as a finite spanning set.

## Implementation notes

**Local instances.** `X.Modules` carries no global monoidal structure, so every section below
reintroduces the monoidal, braided, preadditive and `tensorRight`-additive structures with a
local-instance attribute. The `Stacks.TensorPowers.natTensorPow` transparency attribute is what
makes `powSections L 0` and `(𝟙_ X.Modules ⟶ 𝟙_ X.Modules)` interchangeable for `rw` and for
instance search.

**Why the degree-zero product comes out in the opposite order, and why it does not matter.**
`Stacks.GradedSections.mul s t` is `t ≫ Stacks.GradedSections.mulMap s m`, which in degrees
`0, 0` is the composite `t ≫ s`, while `Stacks.SectionRingGeometric.unitScalar_comp` reads
`unitScalar (b ≫ c) = unitScalar b * unitScalar c`. The two orders are reconciled by
commutativity of `Γ(X, ⊤)`, which is where `map_mul'` of `sectionRingZeroHom` uses `mul_comm`.

**The degree bookkeeping.** `Stacks.TwistedSections.gsmul` lands in degree `n + m` while its
underlying morphism has codomain `F ⊙ (m + n)`, so the computation rule
`Stacks.TwistedSections.of_smul_of` produces a term of degree `0 + p` where the statement wants
`p`. `DirectSum.of_eq_of_gradedMonoid_eq` together with `Stacks.TwistedSections.mk_eq` is the
step that discharges it; no other transport appears.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01CV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01CV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0B5T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B5T)
-/

